 var MFACT2=['James Chadwick discovered the neutron in 1932: a particle with no charge and almost the same mass as a proton.','Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch explained nuclear fission in early 1939. Frisch borrowed the word "fission" from biology, where cells split in two.','Calder Hall in Cumbria opened in 1956 as the world\'s first nuclear power station to supply electricity to a national grid on an industrial scale.','A pressurised water reactor keeps its primary coolant at about 155 bar, so the water stays liquid at over 300 °C.','Uranium is about as common in the Earth\'s crust as tin, and far more common than gold or silver.','Natural uranium is only about 0.7% uranium-235. Most reactor fuel is enriched to around 3 to 5%.','Each fission of uranium-235 releases about 200 MeV: roughly 50 million times the energy of burning one carbon atom.','The blue glow in reactor pools is Cherenkov radiation, made when charged particles travel faster than light can travel through water.','Heavy water, used in CANDU reactors, contains deuterium: hydrogen with an extra neutron in its nucleus.','Control rods often contain boron, cadmium or hafnium, because they are very good at absorbing neutrons.','A sievert measures the health effect of radiation. In the UK the average person gets about 2.7 millisieverts a year.','Chicago Pile-1, the first reactor to run a self-sustaining chain reaction, went critical on 2 December 1942.'];
