 var MFACT=['Marie Curie is the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different sciences: physics in 1903 and chemistry in 1911.','Henri Becquerel discovered radioactivity in 1896, when uranium salts fogged a photographic plate he had wrapped up in a drawer.','Marie Curie came up with the word "radioactivity" in 1898.','Ernest Rutherford named alpha and beta radiation in 1899, after the first two letters of the Greek alphabet.','Neutrons have no electric charge, so they can reach a nucleus without being pushed away. That\'s why they\'re so good at causing fission.','Tritium (hydrogen-3) has a half-life of about 12.3 years.','Carbon dating works because carbon-14 has a half-life of about 5,730 years.','Plutonium-239 is made inside reactors: uranium-238 absorbs a neutron, then beta decays twice.','The Sun turns about 4 million tonnes of mass into energy every second through fusion.','Hinkley Point C in Somerset is being built with two EPR reactors of about 1.6 GW each.','Sizewell B in Suffolk is the only pressurised water reactor running in the UK.','EBR-I in Idaho was the first reactor to make electricity: on 20 December 1951 it lit four light bulbs.'];
