 /* ---------- the Kestrel Point story ---------- */
 var EP=[
 ['First night',['Rain is already hammering the car park when you badge in at Kestrel Point at 21:40. The control room is warmer than you expected, and quieter: just the hum of the panels and a kettle clicking off somewhere.','Priya Nandakumar, the night shift supervisor, doesn\'t look up from the logbook. "You\'re the trainee. Good. Rule one: never rush. Rule two: read every alarm like it\'s the first one you\'ve ever seen."','Reactor 2 is at 100% power, steady as a heartbeat. You spend the evening learning the panels and the people: Rhys from the turbine hall, who brings everyone biscuits, and the shift log, which Priya treats like scripture.','Then, at exactly 03:00, a single amber alarm tile flashes. Before you can even read it, it clears itself.'],'"That," Priya says quietly, "happens every night."',['Priya tests you: an alarm comes in. What do you do first?','Read it, acknowledge it, and check the related indications',['Silence everything so you can think','Trip the reactor straight away','Ignore it if it clears on its own'],'Operators read, acknowledge and verify. An alarm that clears itself still gets investigated.']],
 ['The 03:00 alarm',['The next night you\'re ready, pen in hand. At 03:00 the tile lights again: PRESSURISER LEVEL DEVIATION. Four seconds, then gone.','Rhys leans on the desk. "It\'s the ghost of Reactor 2. Everyone knows."','Priya isn\'t laughing. She pulls the trend up on the big screen and zooms in. The pressuriser level twitches up by a hair, then settles, at the same minute every night for nine nights. "Tiny," she says. "Too tiny to matter. That\'s exactly why it matters."','You stare at the other trends stacked underneath. One of them has the same twitch.'],'At the same moment every night, the coolant temperature dips by 0.3 °C.',['Reactor 2 has a negative temperature coefficient. If the coolant temperature dips, what happens to reactivity?','It goes up a little, so power rises slightly',['It goes down, so power falls','Nothing at all','The reactor trips'],'Cooler water moderates better, so with a negative temperature coefficient reactivity rises. That\'s why a temperature dip shows up as a tiny power blip.']],
 ['Paper trail',['On your day off you can\'t stop thinking about it, so you come in early and ask for the maintenance records.','Most of it is boring in the way safe things are boring: filters changed, pumps tested, lights replaced. Then you find it. A feedwater pump was swapped five weeks ago, and the blips started four days later.','You flick through the paperwork for that job. Tucked into the back of the folder is a yellow sticky note, half peeled off, in someone\'s hurried handwriting.'],'"FW-12 valve sticky. Told them. Nobody listens."',['What does the feedwater system do?','Pumps water into the steam generators to replace the water that boils off as steam',['Pumps coolant through the reactor core','Cools the turbine\'s oil','Sprays seawater on the cooling towers'],'Feedwater keeps the steam generators topped up. If it wobbles, the heat taken from the primary circuit wobbles too, and you see it in the coolant temperature.']],
 ['Rhys\'s theory',['You show Rhys the sticky note and his grin disappears. "FW-12. That\'s on the line to steam generator 2."','He grabs a marker and sketches on the whiteboard. "At 3am demand on the grid is at its lowest, so we back the turbine off a touch. Less steam drawn, the feedwater valves move, and if FW-12 sticks and then jumps…" He taps the board. "Bump."','Priya folds her arms. "Theories are cheap. Data isn\'t. Set up a fast trend on FW-12\'s position for tonight."','You\'re halfway through setting it up when everyone\'s phones buzz at once.'],'The weather warning for tomorrow night has just gone from amber to red.',['The turbine is backed off, so less steam is drawn, but for a moment the reactor makes the same power. What happens to the steam pressure?','It rises',['It falls','It stays exactly the same','The steam instantly turns back into water'],'Same heat in, less steam out, so pressure builds until the control systems rebalance things.']],
 ['Red warning',['The next night the wind sounds like a train that never arrives. Priya gathers the shift around the desk.','"If we lose the grid tonight, here\'s what happens. The turbine trips. The reactor trips automatically. The diesel generators start within seconds and power the safety systems. Our job is to keep the core cool."','She hands you a laminated card: the loss-of-offsite-power procedure. You read it three times. Rhys goes to check the diesels. Outside, something metal clatters across the car park.','At 02:51 the lights dip, recover, and dip again.'],'Then every screen in the room flickers at once.',['If the reactor trips, what still needs cooling?','The fuel, because it keeps making decay heat',['Nothing: the reactor is off','Only the turbine','Only the cooling towers'],'Straight after a trip, decay heat is still around 6–7% of full power. That\'s why cooling has to keep going.']],
 ['Loss of offsite power',['02:52. The grid is gone. The turbine trips with a deep groan you feel through the floor. A heartbeat later the reactor trips: every control rod drops into the core and power falls away.','For two seconds the room is silent except for the alarm horn. Then the diesel generators roar into life, and the emergency lighting turns everyone\'s face orange.','"Read the indications out loud," Priya says, calm as a library. "Rods in. Power falling. Diesels one, two, three running…"','You stop.'],'Diesel generator 4 hasn\'t started.',['When the reactor trips, the control rods…','drop into the core and absorb neutrons, stopping the chain reaction',['are pulled out of the core','melt to plug the core','start pumping coolant'],'The rods fall in under gravity in a couple of seconds and soak up neutrons.']],
 ['Three out of four',['"Three diesels is enough," Priya says, before you can panic. "That\'s why there are four." Rhys is already pulling his coat on to go and look at number 4.','You watch the cooling settle into its new rhythm. The reactor coolant pumps have stopped, so natural circulation carries the heat to the steam generators, and the auxiliary feedwater pumps keep them supplied.','It works. It actually works, just like the textbook. You let out a breath you didn\'t know you were holding. Then you glance at the steam generator levels.'],'Steam generator 2\'s level is falling faster than the other three.',['Why are there four diesel generators when three are enough?','So one can fail and the plant is still safe (redundancy)',['To sell more electricity to the grid','Three is never enough for anything','It\'s just tradition'],'Redundancy means a single failure doesn\'t stop a safety function. It\'s one layer of defence in depth.']],
 ['FW-12',['You trace the line on the mimic panel with your finger. Auxiliary feedwater to steam generator 2 runs through one valve you already know too well: FW-12.','Its position indicator shows it only 20% open. Priya sends the open signal from the panel. Nothing. She sends it again. The valve doesn\'t move.','"It\'s stuck," you say, and your voice sounds very far away. Priya opens the procedure, runs her finger down the page, and looks at you.'],'"Then we do two things at once," she says. "And one of them is you."',['FW-12 is stuck. What\'s the safe thing to do?','Follow the procedure: line up the backup supply to SG2 and send operators to check the valve',['Restart the reactor to make more steam','Wait and see if it fixes itself','Silence the alarms so you can concentrate'],'Procedures exist for exactly this. Use the backup path first, then deal with the fault.']],
 ['Out into the storm',['Priya lines up the backup supply while you and Rhys head out to the valve room. The rain is moving sideways. Even in the corridor you have to shout.','FW-12 sits on the secondary side: a big grey valve with a handwheel the size of a steering wheel. Rhys sets his feet and heaves. Nothing. You both heave. It creaks half a turn, then locks again.','"On three," Rhys gasps. "One… two…"'],'On three there\'s a loud crack, and the handwheel spins free in your hands.',['You and Rhys can work on FW-12 because the secondary water isn\'t normally radioactive. Why not?','It never touches the core: heat passes into it through the steam generator tubes',['It\'s filtered every hour','It\'s colder than the primary water','It\'s seawater'],'The primary and secondary circuits are kept separate. That\'s one of the steam generator\'s main jobs.']],
 ['Steady',['For one horrible second you think you\'ve broken it. Then you hear water rushing through the pipe. The crack was the valve finally freeing itself, and now it\'s wide open.','Your radio crackles. "SG2 level recovering," Priya says. "Nice work, both of you."','You squelch back into the control room, soaked to the skin, and watch the levels climb together. The plant is shut down, stable and cooling. The clock ticks past 03:00. The amber tile stays dark.','Then a different alarm chimes. A cheerful one.'],'GRID SUPPLY RESTORED.',['The grid is back. Before restarting the reactor, what has to happen?','Find and fix the cause, then follow the restart procedure with the right approvals',['Restart straight away while everyone\'s here','Pull all the rods out at once','Skip the checks because it\'s late'],'You never restart into the unknown. Fix it, check it, then restart step by step.']],
 ['The report',['The next afternoon you sit down to write the event report. It\'s harder than the storm.','Priya reads your first draft and slides it back. "Write what happened, not what you wish had happened."','So you write it properly: the times, the alarms, the stuck valve, the diesel that didn\'t start. And the sticky note. Writing that part, you realise someone saw this coming five weeks ago, wrote it down, and it went nowhere. You underline it.'],'An hour later an email arrives: "Please come and see me. Dr Okafor, Station Manager."',['Which sentence belongs in an event report?','At 02:52 offsite power was lost; the reactor tripped automatically and DG4 failed to start',['Everything went a bit wrong but it was fine in the end','Rhys broke the valve','The storm was really scary'],'Reports are factual, timed and specific, so other people can learn from them.']],
 ['The station manager',['Dr Okafor\'s office looks out over the sea, still grey and angry. She has your report open, with the sticky note paragraph highlighted.','"Why do you think this was ignored?" she asks. Your mouth goes dry. You could say you don\'t know.','Instead you say what you think: the note never made it into the system, the person who wrote it felt nobody listened, and the blips looked too small to bother anyone. She\'s quiet for a long moment.'],'"Good," she says. "Then you\'ll present it to the whole station. Friday."',['What does "safety culture" mean?','Everyone feels able to report problems, and problems get listened to',['Never making any mistakes','Wearing hard hats everywhere','Having lots of alarms'],'A strong safety culture means small worries get raised and taken seriously before they become big problems.']],
 ['Friday',['The canteen is packed: engineers, cleaners, managers, and Rhys at the front pulling faces to make you laugh.','You walk them through it: a sticky valve, a nightly blip nobody chased, a note nobody logged, and a storm that turned a small fault into a real test. Then the fixes: FW-12 replaced, and a new rule that every handwritten concern gets logged and answered.','When you finish there\'s a pause, then applause. Afterwards Priya hands you an envelope.'],'"Your first assessment," she says. "Simulator. Tomorrow night."',['In the simulator, reactor power starts creeping up with nobody touching anything. What\'s your first move?','Check what\'s changing and why, then follow the procedure to bring it back',['Pull the rods out to see what happens','Ignore it: it\'s only a simulator','Turn the simulator off'],'Treat the simulator like the real thing: diagnose first, then act by the procedure.']],
 ['The assessment',['The simulator is a perfect copy of the control room, right down to the scuff on the desk. The assessors watch from behind glass.','Twenty minutes in, they throw it at you: main feedwater lost. Your heart thumps, but your hands know what to do. You read the alarms. You check the indications. You confirm the auxiliary feedwater starts. You watch every steam generator level, and you trust nothing you haven\'t checked twice.','When it\'s over, Priya steps out from behind the glass. "Pass," she says. Then, for the first time since you met her, she smiles.'],'End of Season 1. You\'re a Kestrel Point hero.',['Main feedwater is lost. What keeps the steam generators supplied?','The auxiliary (emergency) feedwater system',['The pressuriser','The control rods','The cooling towers'],'Auxiliary feedwater is the backup supply for exactly this. Checking it starts is one of the first things you do.']]];
